WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:10.210
Genomics is extending its reach into diverse
fields of biomedical research from agriculture

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.000
to clinical diagnostics.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.180
Despite sharp falls in recent years, sequencing
costs remain substantial and vary for different

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.000
types of experiment.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.570
Consequently, in all of these fields investigators
are seeking experimental designs that generate

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:29.980
robust scientific findings for the lowest
sequencing cost.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.480
Higher coverage of sequencing inevitably requires
higher costs.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.790
In next-generation sequencing studies, the
term “depth of coverage” refers to how

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:43.579
strong is a genome "covered" by sequenced
fragments (short reads)?

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:48.739
Per-base coverage is the average number of
times a base of a genome is sequenced.

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:53.410
The coverage depth of a genome is calculated
as the number of bases of all short reads

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:57.090
that match a genome divided by the length
of this genome.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:01.630
It is often expressed as 1X, 2X, 3X,... (1,
2, or, 3 times coverage).

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:06.830
“Breadth of coverage” refers to how much
of a genome is "covered" by short reads?

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.920
Are there regions that are not covered, even
not by a single read?

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.030
Breadth of coverage is the percentage of bases
of a reference genome that are covered with

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.090
a certain depth.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:21.430
For example, here is an example for whole
genome sequencing.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:26.390
What coverage of a human genome will one get
with one lane of a HiSeq 2000 in paired-end

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.170
mode, if 200 million clusters were bound?

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.509
In this case, “paired-end” doubles the
read length because each fragment is sequenced

00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:36.450
from both sides.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:41.950
The number of sequenced bases can also be
computed by the number of reads x read length.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:46.450
It should be noted that the computed average
is a theoretical unit.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.119
Reads are not distributed evenly over an entire
genome.

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:54.259
That is an important fact especially for whole
genome sequencing.

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:58.869
Therefore, many bases will be covered by fewer
reads than the average coverage while other

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:03.060
bases will be covered by much more reads than
average.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.689
Coverage histograms are commonly used to depict
the range and uniformity of sequencing coverage

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:09.479
for an entire data set.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.580
They illustrate the overall coverage distribution
by displaying the number of reference bases

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.990
that are covered by mapped sequencing reads
at various depths.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:23.280
Mapped read depth refers to the total number
of bases sequenced and aligned at a given

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.620
reference base position.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.739
In a sequencing coverage histogram, the read
depths are binned and displayed on the x-axis,

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:34.910
while the total numbers of reference bases
that occupy each read depth bin are displayed

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:36.980
on the y-axis.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.530
These can also be written as percentages of
reference bases.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:45.530
Ideally, the plot will take the form of a
Poisson-like distribution with a small standard

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:49.660
deviation, as seen in the left-hand histogram
image.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.800
This distribution is valid under the assumption
that reads are randomly distributed across

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.550
the genome and that the ability to detect
true overlaps between reads is constant within

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:00.190
a sequencing run.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:05.550
However, for a variety of reasons, actual
coverage histograms may have a large spread,

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.370
or have a non-Poisson distribution, as seen
in the right-hand example of a poor sequencing

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.519
coverage histogram.

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:18.590
To evaluate the quality of the NGS coverage,
3 common metrics are usually used:

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:24.930
• Inter-Quartile Range (IQR)
The IQR is the difference in sequencing coverage

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:29.650
between the 75th and 25th percentiles of the
histogram.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:34.769
This value is a measure of statistical variability,
reflecting the non-uniformity of coverage

00:03:34.769 --> 00:03:36.860
across the entire data set.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:42.849
A high IQR indicates high variation in coverage
across the genome, while a low IQR reflects

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:45.780
more uniform sequence coverage.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.400
In the example histograms here, the lower
IQR indicates that the histogram on the left

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.709
has better sequencing coverage uniformity
than that on the right.

00:03:54.709 --> 00:03:59.580
• Mean Read Depth
The mean read depth or mean mapped read depth

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.360
is the sum of the mapped read depths at each
reference base position, divided by the number

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.000
of known bases in the reference.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.909
The mean read depth metric indicates how many
reads, on average, are likely to be aligned

00:04:11.909 --> 00:04:14.740
at a given reference base position.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:19.269
• Raw Read Depth
This is the total amount of sequence data

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:24.770
produced by the instrument during pre-alignment,
divided by the reference genome size.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:30.150
Although raw read depth is often provided
by sequencing instrument vendors as a specification,

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.500
it does not take into account the efficiency
of the alignment process.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.290
If a large fraction of the raw sequencing
reads are discarded during the alignment process,

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:44.750
the post-alignment mapped read depth can be
significantly smaller than the raw read depth.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:50.940
An ideal genome sequencing method would faultlessly
read all nucleotides just once, doing so sequentially

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.120
from one end of a chromosome to the other.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.380
Such a perfect approach would ensure that
all polymorphic alleles within diploid or

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.690
polyploid genomes could be identified, and
that long identical or near-identical repetitive

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:08.010
regions could be unambiguously placed in a
genome assembly.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:13.650
In real-world sequencing approaches, read
lengths are short, often ≤250 nucleotides,

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.889
and can contain sequence errors.

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:22.000
When considered alone, an error is indistinguishable
from a sequence variant.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.760
This problem can be overcome by increasing
the number of sequencing reads.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:31.870
Even if reads contain a 1% variant-error rate,
the combination of eight identical reads that

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:36.080
cover the location of the variant will produce
a strongly supported variant call with an

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.440
associated error rate of 10−16.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:45.430
Increased depth of coverage therefore 'rescues'
inadequacies in sequencing methods.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:50.889
Nevertheless, generating greater depth of
short reads does not cure all sequencing ills.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:56.400
In particular, it alone cannot resolve assembly
gaps that are caused by repetitive regions

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:03.440
with lengths that either approach or exceed
those of the reads.

